(7585551, 1220609, 104230, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by primabarbara:</b></div><hr/><i> I love the patterns! Is it sand? </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks! and yes this is sand. A lot of people spend time nearby and this is as far as they can go in a vehicle, so there are a lot of parking maneuvers "recorded" over time. Once in a while a higher than usual tide washes over the area and the process starts again. <br/> <br/>I inverted the image before doing any other processing.  That helped emphasize the tire patterns.</td>', 'Originally posted by primabarbara: I love the patterns! Is it sand?  Thanks! and yes this is sand. A lot of people spend time nearby and this is as far as they can go in a vehicle, so there are a lot of parking maneuvers "recorded" over time. Once in a while a higher than usual tide washes over the area and the process starts again.  I inverted the image before doing any other processing.  That helped emphasize the tire patterns.', '2018-07-24 23:45:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(7585657, 1220609, 98565, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by PennyClick:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by primabarbara:</b></div><hr/><i> I love the patterns! Is it sand? </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks! and yes this is sand. A lot of people spend time nearby and this is as far as they can go in a vehicle, so there are a lot of parking maneuvers "recorded" over time. Once in a while a higher than usual tide washes over the area and the process starts again. <br/> <br/>I inverted the image before doing any other processing.  That helped emphasize the tire patterns. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for your explanation, Penny. The invertion makes it look like snow on a dark surface. <br/>Awesome pic!</td>', 'Originally posted by PennyClick: Originally posted by primabarbara: I love the patterns! Is it sand?  Thanks! and yes this is sand. A lot of people spend time nearby and this is as far as they can go in a vehicle, so there are a lot of parking maneuvers "recorded" over time. Once in a while a higher than usual tide washes over the area and the process starts again.  I inverted the image before doing any other processing.  That helped emphasize the tire patterns.   Thanks for your explanation, Penny. The invertion makes it look like snow on a dark surface. Awesome pic!', '2018-07-25 05:19:52.000000', NULL, 0),
